really loving something and also seeing it as very profoundly flawed.&quot; -- Jay Eddidin","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2168-chejop-kejak","name":"Chejop Kejak","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2168_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1219"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-25T10:48:20-08:00","id":774115,"title":"","content":"Julius was a son of Mars.<br /> <br /> Fallout: New Vegas even says so!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-25T14:11:12-08:00","id":774271,"title":"","content":"I am convinced that Theodore Roosevelt was a Scion of Odin, probably unvisited","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/267-griffinguy24","name":"griffinguy24","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar267_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-25T14:12:35-08:00","id":774273,"title":"","content":"I joked with a friend the other day about Osamu Tezuka being a Scion of Hachiman.. seeing how much he did for the spread of Manga.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8325-asmael","name":"Asmael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8325_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"89"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-25T15:37:24-08:00","id":774323,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chejop Kejak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/774058-of-whom-was-_______-a-scion#post774058\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Inspired by a thread over in the Exalted side of things, who would the divine parents of assorted historical and pop cultural figures have been?<br /> <br /> On the What We Know wiki, Neall refers to Julius Caesar as a &quot;militant Scion of Venus.&quot; That threw me for a bit of a loop - I understand the desire to shout out to Aeneas, but if there's one thing that Ceasar did <i>not</i> have a great deal of control over, it was his bedroom. Anyone who stays on bad terms with their wife, gets manipulated by Cleopatra, and somehow ends things so poorly with Servillia that her son stabs him is not getting any &quot;love god&quot; points in my book. I know it's a bit trite, but I think I'll shout out to Romulus instead, and assume Caesar was a Scion of Mars.<br /> <br /> That got me thinking about coming up with Scion statuses for his protegees. Marcus is easy - he always had strong associations with the sun (going so far as to name one of his sons Alexander Helios), so Helios (who might be a Mantle of Apollo now?) for him, too. Octavian gave me some pause. His greatest strengths are his people skills and use of information, and Rome doesn't really have an outright Thoth who embodies such ideas. ultimately, I decided that if he ultimately wins out over his many rivals because he has a better core of competent and loyal aides, then nobody shall do save the Goddess of Relationships herself - hail Augustus, son of Hera.<br /> <br /> ... that probably got a bit awkward after Julius became a Demigod himself. So it goes.</div> </div> </div> </div>Caesar spent so much time sleeping around that Cicero mocked him by calling him, &quot;Every man's woman and every woman's man.&quot; Combined with his historically-attested beauty, Scion of Venus seems pretty appropriate to me, particularly since the Julii Caesares like to boast about Venus being part of their lineage.<br /> <br /> I'm not sure what you mean about the rest. He seems to have loved Cinnilla, so much so that he was willing to risk death for her; he divorced Pompeia because of Clodius's Bona Dea-related shenanigans; and he seems to have gotten along fine with Calpurnia. Regarding Cleopatra, he needed someone to replace Ptolemy XIII. Who better than Cleopatra? Particularly since the other candidate was 12 or 13 years old at the time? Finally, what does his relationship with Brutus's mother have to do with Brutus murdering him? If anything, I'd argue that Caesar's tempestuous love life makes him even better-suited as a Scion of Venus, who is not exactly known for having a peaceful love life of her own.<br /> <br /> Regarding the rest, I'd say that Antonius is a Scion of either Hercules or Bacchus. Hercules both because the Antonii claimed to be his descendants and because of Antonius's own physicality. Bacchus because Antonius liked to be worshiped as Dionysus. As for Augustus, I'd go with a Scion of Jupiter, though that's pretty tenuous.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1607-creticus","name":"Creticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"69"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-26T01:03:28-08:00","id":774598,"title":"","content":"As others have said, rich love life doesn't have to mean peaceful or simple, quite the opposite, as Aphrodite's marriage to Hephaestus - and being dragged along with Ares by the same across Olympus in a net after being caught in bed - shows pretty well. Also, roman worship gave a greater emphasis to her aspect as a patroness of war and bringer of victory than the greeks, what was a major element in the julian conection to her, should be said.<br /> <br /> I think there's some stuff from roman times that associated Augustus with Apollo but can't really point out sources at the moment.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1147,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","user":"Baaldam","date":". 11-26-2015, 04:06 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-26T07:46:11-08:00","id":774795,"title":"","content":"Wait, Ares dragged Aphrodite out of bed with a net!?<br /> <br /> Best War God Ever!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-26T08:26:16-08:00","id":774822,"title":"","content":"Nope, it was Hephaestos. Basically, when Hephaestos got back to heaven, Zeus decided that he would marry Aphrodite, simply to stop everyone and everything from trying to woo her. Sadly, Hephaestus wasn't exactyl the man she wished for and so she began multiple affairs, among them one with Ares. <br /> <br /> When Hephaestus found out, he decided to show their shame to everyone and crafted an invisible, yet indestructable net that he placed over his wifes bed and waited. When she and Ares lay down there, the net tightened and caught them both, upon which Hephaestus got all the other gods to look at them. <br /> <br /> Instead of being outraged about this, however, they merely laughted at Hephaestus' expense. Since Homer wrote about this, this is known as &quot;Homeric laughter&quot;, which means long, uncontrollable laughter, often at other peoples misfortune.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8325-asmael","name":"Asmael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8325_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"89"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-26T09:55:02-08:00","id":774879,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/774058-of-whom-was-_______-a-scion?p=774822#post774822\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nope, it was Hephaestos. Basically, when Hephaestos got back to heaven, Zeus decided that he would marry Aphrodite, simply to stop everyone and everything from trying to woo her. Sadly, Hephaestus wasn't exactyl the man she wished for and so she began multiple affairs, among them one with Ares.<br /> <br /> When Hephaestus found out, he decided to show their shame to everyone and crafted an invisible, yet indestructable net that he placed over his wifes bed and waited. When she and Ares lay down there, the net tightened and caught them both, upon which Hephaestus got all the other gods to look at them.<br /> <br /> Instead of being outraged about this, however, they merely laughted at Hephaestus' expense. Since Homer wrote about this, this is known as &quot;Homeric laughter&quot;, which means long, uncontrollable laughter, often at other peoples misfortune.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> I'm getting the impression that the Dodekatheon doesn't give a crap about social justice, especially after the write up for Pan's backstory in Demigod. <br /> <br /> If you're healthy and beautiful, you can get away with murder. If you're ugly or crippled, you're the school punching bag!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-26T11:13:55-08:00","id":774921,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/774058-of-whom-was-_______-a-scion?p=774822#post774822\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nope, it was Hephaestos. Basically, when Hephaestos got back to heaven, Zeus decided that he would marry Aphrodite, simply to stop everyone and everything from trying to woo her. Sadly, Hephaestus wasn't exactyl the man she wished for and so she began multiple affairs, among them one with Ares.<br /> <br /> When Hephaestus found out, he decided to show their shame to everyone and crafted an invisible, yet indestructable net that he placed over his wifes bed and waited. When she and Ares lay down there, the net tightened and caught them both, upon which Hephaestus got all the other gods to look at them.<br /> <br /> Instead of being outraged about this, however, they merely laughted at Hephaestus' expense. Since Homer wrote about this, this is known as &quot;Homeric laughter&quot;, which means long, uncontrollable laughter, often at other peoples misfortune.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, they laughed at the <b>naked lovers</b> expense, as they were being publicly dragged and humiliated by Hephaestus - who was aditionally molified, after some convincing from Poseidon, by being paid an adulterer's fine by Ares and a &quot;bride's price&quot; from Zeus for the revocation of the union.<br /> <br /> As an aside there are tales of Hephaestos having had a number of lovers/consorts and children of his own, so it ain't like he wasn't getting around either.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-26T13:48:15-08:00","id":775005,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Baaldam</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/774058-of-whom-was-_______-a-scion?p=774921#post774921\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, they laughed at the <b>naked lovers</b> expense, as they were being publicly dragged and humiliated by Hephaestus - who was aditionally molified, after some convincing from Poseidon, by being paid an adulterer's fine by Ares and a &quot;bride's price&quot; from Zeus for the revocation of the union.<br /> <br /> As an aside there are tales of Hephaestos having had a number of lovers/consorts and children of his own, so it ain't like he wasn't getting around either.</div> </div> </div> </div>Interestingly, I've seen both versions of this story enough times that I think both were probably told in antiquity.<br /> <br /> ... heck, that seems pretty true to how different people relate stories of revenge and humiliation among spouses <i>today</i>,<br /> <br /> (But, no, Greek Gods don't give a dang about social justice. Neither does almost anyone from almost any ancient culture. It's pretty rough.)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;For me, there's no fundamental conflict between really loving something and also seeing it as very profoundly flawed.&quot; -- Jay Eddidin","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2168-chejop-kejak","name":"Chejop Kejak","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2168_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1219"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-26T13:55:16-08:00","id":775014,"title":"","content":"Theres the version Baaldam mentioned, too, but its rarely told. At least it is believed to have been told that way only about 100 years after the one I mentioned. Antique stories often have several iterations, no matter where they are from.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8325-asmael","name":"Asmael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8325_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"89"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-26T22:21:01-08:00","id":775261,"title":"","content":"By the way, I just realized something. A lot of people like to think Zeus got the lion's share when he and his brothers divided up their power, but upon retrospect, I think he actually got the raw end of the deal.<br /> <br /> <br /> Poseidon become the god of the sea, instantly claiming dominion over 2/3 of the planet. He's also the god of earthquakes and could very easily destroy the world any time he wanted.<br /> <br /> Hades became god of the underworld and wealth. Claiming dominion over departed souls and all the earth's resources.<br /> <br /> Zeus became god of the sky and lightning. That's about it, he rules over empty space and somehow that makes him worthy to be king of the gods.<br /> <br /> <br /> So while Zeus is off making a show about how comfy his throne is, Poseidon and Hades are the ones who have any real power over the world!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 11-27-2015, 09:26 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-27T02:26:56-08:00","id":775331,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/774058-of-whom-was-_______-a-scion?p=775261#post775261\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">By the way, I just realized something. A lot of people like to think Zeus got the lion's share when he and his brothers divided up their power, but upon retrospect, I think he actually got the raw end of the deal.<br /> <br /> <br /> Poseidon become the got of the sea, instantly claiming dominion over 2/3 of the planet. He's also the god of earthquakes and could very easily destroy the world any time he wanted.<br /> <br /> Hades became god of the underworld and wealth. Claiming dominion over departed souls and all the earth's resources.<br /> <br /> Zeus became god of the sky and lightning. That's about it, he rules over empty space and somehow that makes him worthy to be king of the gods.<br /> <br /> <br /> So while Zeus is off making a show about how comfy his throne is, Poseidon and Hades are the ones who have any real power over the world!</div> </div> </div> </div>What also makes him the master of all weather, a pretty huge deal in the lives of ancient people and assuring him a preferential place when it comes to propitiation, should be said.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-01T14:01:11-08:00","id":777907,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/774058-of-whom-was-_______-a-scion?p=775261#post775261\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">By the way, I just realized something. A lot of people like to think Zeus got the lion's share when he and his brothers divided up their power, but upon retrospect, I think he actually got the raw end of the deal.<br /> <br /> <br /> Poseidon become the god of the sea, instantly claiming dominion over 2/3 of the planet. He's also the god of earthquakes and could very easily destroy the world any time he wanted.<br /> <br /> Hades became god of the underworld and wealth. Claiming dominion over departed souls and all the earth's resources.<br /> <br /> Zeus became god of the sky and lightning. That's about it, he rules over empty space and somehow that makes him worthy to be king of the gods.<br /> <br /> <br /> So while Zeus is off making a show about how comfy his throne is, Poseidon and Hades are the ones who have any real power over the world!</div> </div> </div> </div>IIRC, Zeus claimed and none of the gods disagreed that he could defeat any of them at any time, and none could stop him, and he also claimed that if he challenged them all to tug of war, the entire pantheon together would not be able to defeat him, and none of the gods including his brothers or sisters disagreed. So yes he was the most powerful, in the mythology. By a lot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1919-muadib","name":"muadib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1919_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-01T18:45:33-08:00","id":778023,"title":"","content":"Or they were probably just humoring the guy who decided to claim dominion over a bunch of puffy clouds. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> Zeus: &quot;I am the greatest God in the world!&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> Poseidon and Hades: &quot;Yes, Zeus, that's very nice. But we have to get back to work.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-02T14:55:22-08:00","id":778609,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>muadib</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/774058-of-whom-was-_______-a-scion?p=777907#post777907\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">IIRC, Zeus claimed and none of the gods disagreed that he could defeat any of them at any time, and none could stop him, and he also claimed that if he challenged them all to tug of war, the entire pantheon together would not be able to defeat him, and none of the gods including his brothers or sisters disagreed. So yes he was the most powerful, in the mythology. By a lot.</div> </div> </div> </div>As with most myths, this comes in several flavors. For example, the version with which I am most familiar has the three competing... and Zeus comes in second. Poseidon was the strongest, but also kind of an antisocial prick, so he didn't want to be leader of the gods, and instead set up his own kingdom outside of Zeus' dominion. Hades lost outright, so he got what nobody else wanted.<br /> <br /> But then again, there are plenty of other stories which do show Poseidon cowed by Zeus' authority, so take what you need as your personal canon.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;For me, there's no fundamental conflict between really loving something and also seeing it as very profoundly flawed.&quot; -- Jay Eddidin","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2168-chejop-kejak","name":"Chejop Kejak","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2168_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1219"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-03T17:26:26-08:00","id":779375,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chejop Kejak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/774058-of-whom-was-_______-a-scion?p=778609#post778609\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As with most myths, this comes in several flavors. For example, the version with which I am most familiar has the three competing... and Zeus comes in second. Poseidon was the strongest, but also kind of an antisocial prick, so he didn't want to be leader of the gods, and instead set up his own kingdom outside of Zeus' dominion. Hades lost outright, so he got what nobody else wanted.<br /> <br /> But then again, there are plenty of other stories which do show Poseidon cowed by Zeus' authority, so take what you need as your personal canon.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Wait, I thought Hades was the anti-social one. He chose the underworld because it would isolate him from people. Heck, he even has a helmet that renders him invisible. For somebody claiming to be anti-social, Poseidon sure did interact with people a lot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-03T18:33:44-08:00","id":779422,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chejop Kejak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/774058-of-whom-was-_______-a-scion?p=778609#post778609\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As with most myths, this comes in several flavors. For example, the version with which I am most familiar has the three competing... and Zeus comes in second. Poseidon was the strongest, but also kind of an antisocial prick, so he didn't want to be leader of the gods, and instead set up his own kingdom outside of Zeus' dominion. Hades lost outright, so he got what nobody else wanted.<br /> <br /> But then again, there are plenty of other stories which do show Poseidon cowed by Zeus' authority, so take what you need as your personal canon.</div> </div> </div> </div>Eh, i tend to run with Homer as my go to,and as a result felt that Zeus was lowballed by 1st ed. But thats purely my preference","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1919-muadib","name":"muadib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1919_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-03T19:00:38-08:00","id":779448,"title":"","content":"I tend to go with Homer as well so I re-wrote all of Greeks and filled out their ranks. But then again I guess every ST does that. We all have ideas of how the gods are stated out in our heads if they interest us at all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/333-achilles","name":"Achilles","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar333_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"81"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}